      {
        "id": "downwelling-longwave-flux-in-air-assuming-clear-sky-and-reference-mole-fraction-of-ozone-in-air",
        "validation-key": "downwelling-longwave-flux-in-air-assuming-clear-sky-and-reference-mole-fraction-of-ozone-in-air",
        "ui-label": "Downwelling Longwave Flux In Air Assuming Clear Sky And Reference Mole Fraction Of Ozone In Air",
        "description": "Downwelling radiation is radiation from above. It does not mean \"net downward\". The sign convention is that \"upwelling\" is positive upwards and \"downwelling\" is positive downwards. The term \"longwave\" means longwave radiation. When thought of as being incident on a surface, a radiative flux is sometimes called \"irradiance\". In addition, it is identical with the quantity measured by a cosine-collector light-meter and sometimes called \"vector irradiance\". In accordance with common usage in geophysical disciplines, \"flux\" implies per unit area, called \"flux density\" in physics. A phrase assuming_condition indicates that the named quantity is the value which would obtain if all aspects of the system were unaltered except for the assumption of the circumstances specified by the condition. \"Clear sky\" means in the absence of clouds. This 3D ozone field acts as a reference ozone field in a diagnostic call to the model's radiation scheme. It is expressed in terms of mole fraction of ozone in air. It may be observation-based or model-derived. It may be from any time period. By using the same ozone reference in the diagnostic radiation call in two model simulations and calculating differences between the radiative flux diagnostics from the prognostic call to the radiation scheme and the diagnostic call to the radiation scheme with the ozone reference, an instantaneous radiative forcing for ozone can be calculated.",
        "canonical_units": "W m-2",
        "cf-name": "downwelling_longwave_flux_in_air_assuming_clear_sky_and_reference_mole_fraction_of_ozone_in_air",
        "type": [
          "wcrp:standard-name",
          "cf"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "downwelling-longwave-flux-in-air-assuming-clear-sky",
        "validation-key": "downwelling-longwave-flux-in-air-assuming-clear-sky",
        "ui-label": "Downwelling Longwave Flux In Air Assuming Clear Sky",
        "description": "Downwelling radiation is radiation from above. It does not mean \"net downward\". The sign convention is that \"upwelling\" is positive upwards and \"downwelling\" is positive downwards. The term \"longwave\" means longwave radiation. When thought of as being incident on a surface, a radiative flux is sometimes called \"irradiance\". In addition, it is identical with the quantity measured by a cosine-collector light-meter and sometimes called \"vector irradiance\". In accordance with common usage in geophysical disciplines, \"flux\" implies per unit area, called \"flux density\" in physics. A phrase assuming_condition indicates that the named quantity is the value which would obtain if all  aspects of the system were unaltered except for the assumption of the  circumstances specified by the condition. \"Clear sky\" means in the absence of clouds.",
        "canonical_units": "W m-2",
        "cf-name": "downwelling_longwave_flux_in_air_assuming_clear_sky",
        "type": [
          "wcrp:standard-name",
          "cf"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "downwelling-longwave-flux-in-air-assuming-reference-mole-fraction-of-ozone-in-air",
        "validation-key": "downwelling-longwave-flux-in-air-assuming-reference-mole-fraction-of-ozone-in-air",
        "ui-label": "Downwelling Longwave Flux In Air Assuming Reference Mole Fraction Of Ozone In Air",
        "description": "Downwelling radiation is radiation from above. It does not mean \"net downward\". The sign convention is that \"upwelling\" is positive upwards and \"downwelling\" is positive downwards. The term \"longwave\" means longwave radiation. When thought of as being incident on a surface, a radiative flux is sometimes called \"irradiance\". In addition, it is identical with the quantity measured by a cosine-collector light-meter and sometimes called \"vector irradiance\". In accordance with common usage in geophysical disciplines, \"flux\" implies per unit area, called \"flux density\" in physics. A phrase assuming_condition indicates that the named quantity is the value which would obtain if all aspects of the system were unaltered except for the assumption of the circumstances specified by the condition. This 3D ozone field acts as a reference ozone field in a diagnostic call to the model's radiation scheme. It is expressed in terms of mole fraction of ozone in air. It may be observation-based or model-derived. It may be from any time period. By using the same ozone reference in the diagnostic radiation call in two model simulations and calculating differences between the radiative flux diagnostics from the prognostic call to the radiation scheme and the diagnostic call to the radiation scheme with the ozone reference, an instantaneous radiative forcing for ozone can be calculated.",
        "canonical_units": "W m-2",
        "cf-name": "downwelling_longwave_flux_in_air_assuming_reference_mole_fraction_of_ozone_in_air",
        "type": [
          "wcrp:standard-name",
          "cf"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "downwelling-longwave-flux-in-air",
        "validation-key": "downwelling-longwave-flux-in-air",
        "ui-label": "Downwelling Longwave Flux In Air",
        "description": "Downwelling radiation is radiation from above. It does not mean \"net downward\". The sign convention is that \"upwelling\" is positive upwards and \"downwelling\" is positive downwards. The term \"longwave\" means longwave radiation. When thought of as being incident on a surface, a radiative flux is sometimes called \"irradiance\". In addition, it is identical with the quantity measured by a cosine-collector light-meter and sometimes called \"vector irradiance\". In accordance with common usage in geophysical disciplines, \"flux\" implies per unit area, called \"flux density\" in physics.",
        "canonical_units": "W m-2",
        "cf-name": "downwelling_longwave_flux_in_air",
        "type": [
          "wcrp:standard-name",
          "cf"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "downwelling-longwave-radiance-in-air",
        "validation-key": "downwelling-longwave-radiance-in-air",
        "ui-label": "Downwelling Longwave Radiance In Air",
        "description": "Downwelling radiation is radiation from above. It does not mean \"net downward\". The sign convention is that \"upwelling\" is positive upwards and \"downwelling\" is positive downwards. The term \"longwave\" means longwave radiation. Radiance is the radiative flux in a particular direction, per unit of solid angle. The direction from which it is coming must be specified, for instance with a coordinate of zenith_angle. If the radiation does not depend on direction, a standard name of isotropic radiance should be chosen instead.",
        "canonical_units": "W m-2 sr-1",
        "cf-name": "downwelling_longwave_radiance_in_air",
        "type": [
          "wcrp:standard-name",
          "cf"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "downwelling-photon-flux-in-sea-water",
        "validation-key": "downwelling-photon-flux-in-sea-water",
        "ui-label": "Downwelling Photon Flux In Sea Water",
        "description": "Downwelling radiation is radiation from above. It does not mean \"net downward\". The sign convention is that \"upwelling\" is positive upwards and \"downwelling\" is positive downwards. A photon flux is specified in terms of numbers of photons expressed in moles. In accordance with common usage in geophysical disciplines, \"flux\" implies per unit area, called \"flux density\" in physics.",
        "canonical_units": "mol m-2 s-1",
        "cf-name": "downwelling_photon_flux_in_sea_water",
        "type": [
          "wcrp:standard-name",
          "cf"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "downwelling-photon-flux-per-unit-wavelength-in-sea-water",
        "validation-key": "downwelling-photon-flux-per-unit-wavelength-in-sea-water",
        "ui-label": "Downwelling Photon Flux Per Unit Wavelength In Sea Water",
        "description": "Downwelling radiation is radiation from above. It does not mean \"net downward\". The sign convention is that \"upwelling\" is positive upwards and \"downwelling\" is positive downwards. A photon flux is specified in terms of numbers of photons expressed in moles. In accordance with common usage in geophysical disciplines, \"flux\" implies per unit area, called \"flux density\" in physics. A coordinate variable for radiation wavelength should be given the standard name radiation_wavelength.",
        "canonical_units": "mol m-2 s-1 m-1",
        "cf-name": "downwelling_photon_flux_per_unit_wavelength_in_sea_water",
        "type": [
          "wcrp:standard-name",
          "cf"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "downwelling-photon-radiance-in-sea-water",
        "validation-key": "downwelling-photon-radiance-in-sea-water",
        "ui-label": "Downwelling Photon Radiance In Sea Water",
        "description": "Downwelling radiation is radiation from above. It does not mean \"net downward\". The sign convention is that \"upwelling\" is positive upwards and \"downwelling\" is positive downwards. Photon radiance is the photon flux in a particular direction, per unit of solid angle. The direction from which it is coming must be specified, for instance with a coordinate of zenith_angle. If the radiation does not depend on direction, a standard name of isotropic radiance should be chosen instead. A photon flux is specified in terms of numbers of photons expressed in moles.",
        "canonical_units": "mol m-2 s-1 sr-1",
        "cf-name": "downwelling_photon_radiance_in_sea_water",
        "type": [
          "wcrp:standard-name",
          "cf"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "downwelling-photon-radiance-per-unit-wavelength-in-sea-water",
        "validation-key": "downwelling-photon-radiance-per-unit-wavelength-in-sea-water",
        "ui-label": "Downwelling Photon Radiance Per Unit Wavelength In Sea Water",
        "description": "Downwelling radiation is radiation from above. It does not mean \"net downward\". The sign convention is that \"upwelling\" is positive upwards and \"downwelling\" is positive downwards. Photon radiance is the photon flux in a particular direction, per unit of solid angle. The direction from which it is coming must be specified, for instance with a coordinate of zenith_angle. If the radiation does not depend on direction, a standard name of isotropic radiance should be chosen instead. A photon flux is specified in terms of numbers of photons expressed in moles. A coordinate variable for radiation wavelength should be given the standard name radiation_wavelength.",
        "canonical_units": "mol m-2 s-1 m-1 sr-1",
        "cf-name": "downwelling_photon_radiance_per_unit_wavelength_in_sea_water",
        "type": [
          "wcrp:standard-name",
          "cf"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "downwelling-photon-spherical-irradiance-in-sea-water",
        "validation-key": "downwelling-photon-spherical-irradiance-in-sea-water",
        "ui-label": "Downwelling Photon Spherical Irradiance In Sea Water",
        "description": "Downwelling radiation is radiation from above. It does not mean \"net downward\". The sign convention is that \"upwelling\" is positive upwards and \"downwelling\" is positive downwards. Photon spherical irradiance is the photon flux incident on unit area of a hemispherical (or \"2-pi\") collector. Radiation incident on a 4-pi collector has a standard name referring to \"omnidirectional spherical irradiance\". A photon flux is specified in terms of numbers of photons expressed in moles.",
        "canonical_units": "mol m-2 s-1",
        "cf-name": "downwelling_photon_spherical_irradiance_in_sea_water",
        "type": [
          "wcrp:standard-name",
          "cf"
        ]
      },
      {
