        "id": "northward-water-vapor-transport-across-unit-distance-in-atmosphere-layer",
        "validation-key": "northward-water-vapor-transport-across-unit-distance-in-atmosphere-layer",
        "ui-label": "Northward Water Vapor Transport Across Unit Distance In Atmosphere Layer",
        "description": "\"Layer\" means any layer with upper and lower boundaries that have constant values in some vertical coordinate. There must be a vertical coordinate variable indicating the extent of the layer(s). If the layers are model layers, the vertical coordinate can be model_level_number, but it is recommended to specify a physical coordinate (in a scalar or auxiliary coordinate variable) as well. \"Northward\" indicates a vector component which is positive when directed northward (negative southward). Transport across_unit_distance means expressed per unit distance normal to the direction of transport.",
        "canonical_units": "kg m-1 s-1",
        "cf-name": "northward_water_vapor_transport_across_unit_distance_in_atmosphere_layer",
        "type": [
          "wcrp:standard-name",
          "cf"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "northward-westward-derivative-of-geopotential",
        "validation-key": "northward-westward-derivative-of-geopotential",
        "ui-label": "Northward Westward Derivative Of Geopotential",
        "description": "A quantity with standard name Xward_Yward_derivative_of_geopotential is a second spatial derivative of geopotential in the direction specified by X and Y, i.e., d2P/dXdY. Geopotential is the sum of the specific gravitational potential energy relative to the geoid and the specific centripetal potential energy. \"Westward\" indicates a vector component which is positive when directed westward (negative eastward). \"Northward\" indicates a vector component which is positive when directed northward (negative southward). \"component_derivative_of_X\" means derivative of X with respect to distance in the component direction, which may be \"northward\", \"southward\", \"eastward\", \"westward\", \"x\" or \"y\". The last two indicate derivatives along the axes of the grid, in the case where they are not true longitude and latitude.",
        "canonical_units": "s-2",
        "cf-name": "northward_westward_derivative_of_geopotential",
        "type": [
          "wcrp:standard-name",
          "cf"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "northward-wind",
        "validation-key": "northward-wind",
        "ui-label": "Northward Wind",
        "description": "\"Northward\" indicates a vector component which is positive when directed northward (negative southward). Wind is defined as a two-dimensional (horizontal) air velocity vector, with no vertical component. (Vertical motion in the atmosphere has the standard name upward_air_velocity.)",
        "canonical_units": "m s-1",
        "cf-name": "northward_wind",
        "type": [
          "wcrp:standard-name",
          "cf"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "nudging-increment-in-mass-content-of-water-in-soil",
        "validation-key": "nudging-increment-in-mass-content-of-water-in-soil",
        "ui-label": "Nudging Increment In Mass Content Of Water In Soil",
        "description": "A \"nudging increment\" refers to an amount added to parts of a model system. The phrase \"nudging_increment_in_X\" refers to an increment in quantity X over a time period which should be defined in the bounds of the time coordinate. \"Content\" indicates a quantity per unit area. \"Water\" means water in all phases. The mass content of water in soil refers to the vertical integral from the surface down to the bottom of the soil model. For the content between specified levels in the soil, standard names including \"content_of_soil_layer\" are used.",
        "canonical_units": "kg m-2",
        "cf-name": "nudging_increment_in_mass_content_of_water_in_soil",
        "type": [
          "wcrp:standard-name",
          "cf"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "nudging-increment-in-snow-and-ice-amount-on-land",
        "validation-key": "nudging-increment-in-snow-and-ice-amount-on-land",
        "ui-label": "Nudging Increment In Snow And Ice Amount On Land",
        "description": "A \"nudging increment\" refers to an amount added to parts of a model system. The phrase \"nudging_increment_in_X\" refers to an increment in quantity X over a time period which should be defined in the bounds of the time coordinate. \"Amount\" means mass per unit area. \"Snow and ice on land\" means ice in glaciers, ice caps, ice sheets & shelves, river and lake ice, any other ice on a land surface, such as frozen flood water, and snow lying on such ice or on the land surface.",
        "canonical_units": "kg m-2",
        "cf-name": "nudging_increment_in_snow_and_ice_amount_on_land",
        "type": [
          "wcrp:standard-name",
          "cf"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "number-concentration-of-aerosol-particles-at-stp-in-air",
        "validation-key": "number-concentration-of-aerosol-particles-at-stp-in-air",
        "ui-label": "Number Concentration Of Aerosol Particles At Stp In Air",
        "description": "\"Number concentration\" means the number of particles or other specified objects per unit volume. \"Aerosol\" means the system of suspended liquid or solid particles in air (except cloud droplets) and their carrier gas, the air itself. \"stp\" means standard temperature (0 degC) and pressure (101325 Pa). The surface called \"surface\" means the lower boundary of the atmosphere.",
        "canonical_units": "m-3",
        "cf-name": "number_concentration_of_aerosol_particles_at_stp_in_air",
        "type": [
          "wcrp:standard-name",
          "cf"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "number-concentration-of-aerosol-particles-in-air",
        "validation-key": "number-concentration-of-aerosol-particles-in-air",
        "ui-label": "Number Concentration Of Aerosol Particles In Air",
        "description": "\"Number concentration\" means the number of particles or other specified objects per unit volume. \"Aerosol\" means the system of suspended liquid or solid particles in air (except cloud droplets) and their carrier gas, the air itself.",
        "canonical_units": "m-3",
        "cf-name": "number_concentration_of_aerosol_particles_in_air",
        "type": [
          "wcrp:standard-name",
          "cf"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "number-concentration-of-ambient-aerosol-particles-in-air",
        "validation-key": "number-concentration-of-ambient-aerosol-particles-in-air",
        "ui-label": "Number Concentration Of Ambient Aerosol Particles In Air",
        "description": "\"Number concentration\" means the number of particles or other specified objects per unit volume. \"Aerosol\" means the system of suspended liquid or solid particles in air (except cloud droplets) and their carrier gas, the air itself. \"Ambient_aerosol\" means that the aerosol is measured or modelled at the ambient state of pressure, temperature and relative humidity that exists in its immediate environment. \"Ambient aerosol particles\" are aerosol particles that have taken up ambient water through hygroscopic growth. The extent of hygroscopic growth depends on the relative humidity and the composition of the particles.",
        "canonical_units": "m-3",
        "cf-name": "number_concentration_of_ambient_aerosol_particles_in_air",
        "type": [
          "wcrp:standard-name",
          "cf"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "number-concentration-of-biological-taxon-in-sea-water",
        "validation-key": "number-concentration-of-biological-taxon-in-sea-water",
        "ui-label": "Number Concentration Of Biological Taxon In Sea Water",
        "description": "\"Number concentration\" means the number of particles or other specified objects per unit volume. \"Biological taxon\" is a name or other label identifying an organism or a group of organisms as belonging to a unit of classification in a hierarchical taxonomy. There must be an auxiliary coordinate variable with standard name biological_taxon_name to identify the taxon in human readable format and optionally an auxiliary coordinate variable with standard name biological_taxon_lsid to provide a  machine-readable identifier. See Section 6.1.2 of the CF convention (version 1.8 or later) for information about biological taxon auxiliary coordinate variables.",
        "canonical_units": "m-3",
        "cf-name": "number_concentration_of_biological_taxon_in_sea_water",
        "type": [
          "wcrp:standard-name",
          "cf"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "number-concentration-of-biological-taxon-pollen-grains-in-air",
        "validation-key": "number-concentration-of-biological-taxon-pollen-grains-in-air",
        "ui-label": "Number Concentration Of Biological Taxon Pollen Grains In Air",
        "description": "\"Number concentration\" means the number of particles or other specified objects per unit volume. \"Pollen grain\" refers to the male gametophyte of seed plants (either angiosperms or gymnosperms). The number concentration of pollen grains refers to the number of individual pollen grains per unit volume. \"Biological taxon\" is a name or other label identifying an organism or a group of organisms as belonging to a unit of classification in a hierarchical taxonomy. There must be an auxiliary coordinate variable with standard name biological_taxon_name to identify the taxon in human readable format and optionally an auxiliary coordinate variable with standard name biological_taxon_identifier to provide a machine-readable identifier. See Section 6.1.2 of the CF convention (version 1.8 or later) for information about biological taxon auxiliary coordinate variables.",
        "canonical_units": "m-3",
        "cf-name": "number_concentration_of_biological_taxon_pollen_grains_in_air",
        "type": [
          "wcrp:standard-name",
          "cf"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "number-concentration-of-cloud-condensation-nuclei-assuming-reference-relative-humidity",
        "validation-key": "number-concentration-of-cloud-condensation-nuclei-assuming-reference-relative-humidity",
        "ui-label": "Number Concentration Of Cloud Condensation Nuclei Assuming Reference Relative Humidity",
        "description": "\"Number concentration\" means the number of particles or other specified objects per unit volume. The cloud condensation nuclei number concentration is the total number of aerosol particles per unit volume independent of and integrated over particle size that act as condensation nuclei for liquid-phase clouds. A coordinate variable with the standard name of relative_humidity should be specified to indicate that the property refers to a specific supersaturation with respect to liquid water. The ability of a particle to act as a condensation nucleus is determined by its size, chemical composition, and morphology.",
        "canonical_units": "m-3",
        "cf-name": "number_concentration_of_cloud_condensation_nuclei_assuming_reference_relative_humidity",
        "type": [
          "wcrp:standard-name",
          "cf"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "number-concentration-of-cloud-condensation-nuclei-at-stp-in-air",
        "validation-key": "number-concentration-of-cloud-condensation-nuclei-at-stp-in-air",
        "ui-label": "Number Concentration Of Cloud Condensation Nuclei At Stp In Air",
        "description": "The cloud condensation nuclei number concentration is the total number of aerosol particles per unit volume independent of and integrated over particle size that act as condensation nuclei for liquid-phase clouds. A coordinate variable with the standard name of relative_humidity should be specified to indicate that the property refers to a specific supersaturation with respect to liquid water. The ability of a particle to act as a condensation nucleus is determined by its size, chemical composition, and morphology. \"stp\" means standard temperature (0 degC) and pressure (101325 Pa).",
        "canonical_units": "m-3",
        "cf-name": "number_concentration_of_cloud_condensation_nuclei_at_stp_in_air",
        "type": [
          "wcrp:standard-name",
          "cf"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "number-concentration-of-cloud-condensation-nuclei-in-air",
        "validation-key": "number-concentration-of-cloud-condensation-nuclei-in-air",
        "ui-label": "Number Concentration Of Cloud Condensation Nuclei In Air",
        "description": "\"Number concentration\" means the number of particles or other specified objects per unit volume. The cloud condensation nuclei number concentration is the total number of aerosol particles per unit volume independent of and integrated over particle size that act as condensation nuclei for liquid-phase clouds. A coordinate variable with the standard name of relative_humidity should be specified to indicate that the property refers to a specific supersaturation with respect to liquid water. The ability of a particle to act as a condensation nucleus is determined by its size, chemical composition, and morphology.",
        "canonical_units": "m-3",
        "cf-name": "number_concentration_of_cloud_condensation_nuclei_in_air",
        "type": [
          "wcrp:standard-name",
          "cf"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "number-concentration-of-cloud-liquid-water-particles-in-air-at-liquid-water-cloud-top",
        "validation-key": "number-concentration-of-cloud-liquid-water-particles-in-air-at-liquid-water-cloud-top",
        "ui-label": "Number Concentration Of Cloud Liquid Water Particles In Air At Liquid Water Cloud Top",
        "description": "\"Number concentration\" means the number of particles or other specified objects per unit volume. \"Cloud liquid water\" refers to the liquid phase of cloud water. A diameter of 0.2 mm has been suggested as an upper limit to the size of drops that shall be regarded as cloud drops; larger drops fall rapidly enough so that only very strong updrafts can sustain them. Any such division is somewhat arbitrary, and active cumulus clouds sometimes contain cloud drops much larger than this. Reference: AMS Glossary http://glossary.ametsoc.org/wiki/Cloud_drop. cloud_top refers to the top of the highest cloud.",
        "canonical_units": "m-3",
        "cf-name": "number_concentration_of_cloud_liquid_water_particles_in_air_at_liquid_water_cloud_top",
        "type": [
          "wcrp:standard-name",
          "cf"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "number-concentration-of-cloud-liquid-water-particles-in-air",
        "validation-key": "number-concentration-of-cloud-liquid-water-particles-in-air",
        "ui-label": "Number Concentration Of Cloud Liquid Water Particles In Air",
        "description": "\"Number concentration\" means the number of particles or other specified objects per unit volume. \"Cloud liquid water\" refers to the liquid phase of cloud water. A diameter of 0.2 mm has been suggested as an upper limit to the size of drops that shall be regarded as cloud drops; larger drops fall rapidly enough so that only very strong updrafts can sustain them. Any such division is somewhat arbitrary, and active cumulus clouds sometimes contain cloud drops much larger than this. Reference: AMS Glossary http://glossary.ametsoc.org/wiki/Cloud_drop.",
        "canonical_units": "m-3",
        "cf-name": "number_concentration_of_cloud_liquid_water_particles_in_air",
        "type": [
          "wcrp:standard-name",
          "cf"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "number-concentration-of-coarse-mode-ambient-aerosol-particles-in-air",
        "validation-key": "number-concentration-of-coarse-mode-ambient-aerosol-particles-in-air",
        "ui-label": "Number Concentration Of Coarse Mode Ambient Aerosol Particles In Air",
        "description": "\"Number concentration\" means the number of particles or other specified objects per unit volume. \"Aerosol\" means the system of suspended liquid or solid particles in air (except cloud droplets) and their carrier gas, the air itself. \"Ambient_aerosol\" means that the aerosol is measured or modelled at the ambient state of pressure, temperature and relative humidity that exists in its immediate environment. \"Ambient aerosol particles\" are aerosol particles that have taken up ambient water through hygroscopic growth. The extent of hygroscopic growth depends on the relative humidity and the composition of the particles. Coarse mode aerosol particles have a diameter of more than 1 micrometer.",
        "canonical_units": "m-3",
        "cf-name": "number_concentration_of_coarse_mode_ambient_aerosol_particles_in_air",
        "type": [
          "wcrp:standard-name",
          "cf"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "number-concentration-of-convective-cloud-liquid-water-particles-at-convective-liquid-water-cloud-top",
        "validation-key": "number-concentration-of-convective-cloud-liquid-water-particles-at-convective-liquid-water-cloud-top",
        "ui-label": "Number Concentration Of Convective Cloud Liquid Water Particles At Convective Liquid Water Cloud Top",
        "description": "\"Number concentration\" means the number of particles or other specified objects per unit volume. The phrase \"convective_liquid_water_cloud_top\" refers to the top of the highest convective liquid water cloud. Convective cloud is that produced by the convection schemes in an atmosphere model.  \"Cloud liquid water\" refers to the liquid phase of cloud water. A diameter of 0.2 mm has been suggested as an upper limit to the size of drops that shall be regarded as cloud drops; larger drops fall rapidly enough so that only very strong updrafts can sustain them. Any such division is somewhat arbitrary, and active cumulus clouds sometimes contain cloud drops much larger than this. Reference: AMS Glossary http://glossary.ametsoc.org/wiki/Cloud_drop.",
        "canonical_units": "m-3",
        "cf-name": "number_concentration_of_convective_cloud_liquid_water_particles_at_convective_liquid_water_cloud_top",
        "type": [
          "wcrp:standard-name",
          "cf"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "number-concentration-of-ice-crystals-in-air-at-ice-cloud-top",
        "validation-key": "number-concentration-of-ice-crystals-in-air-at-ice-cloud-top",
        "ui-label": "Number Concentration Of Ice Crystals In Air At Ice Cloud Top",
        "description": "\"Number concentration\" means the number of particles or other specified objects per unit volume. cloud_top refers to the top of the highest cloud.",
        "canonical_units": "m-3",
        "cf-name": "number_concentration_of_ice_crystals_in_air_at_ice_cloud_top",
        "type": [
          "wcrp:standard-name",
          "cf"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "number-concentration-of-ice-crystals-in-air",
        "validation-key": "number-concentration-of-ice-crystals-in-air",
        "ui-label": "Number Concentration Of Ice Crystals In Air",
        "description": "\"Number concentration\" means the number of particles or other specified objects per unit volume.",
        "canonical_units": "m-3",
        "cf-name": "number_concentration_of_ice_crystals_in_air",
        "type": [
          "wcrp:standard-name",
          "cf"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "number-concentration-of-nucleation-mode-ambient-aerosol-particles-in-air",
        "validation-key": "number-concentration-of-nucleation-mode-ambient-aerosol-particles-in-air",
        "ui-label": "Number Concentration Of Nucleation Mode Ambient Aerosol Particles In Air",
        "description": "\"Number concentration\" means the number of particles or other specified objects per unit volume. \"Ambient_aerosol\" means that the aerosol is measured or modelled at the ambient state of pressure, temperature and relative humidity that exists in its immediate environment. \"Ambient aerosol particles\" are aerosol particles that have taken up ambient water through hygroscopic growth. The extent of hygroscopic growth depends on the relative humidity and the composition of the particles. Nucleation mode aerosol particles have a diameter of less than 3 nanometers.",
        "canonical_units": "m-3",
        "cf-name": "number_concentration_of_nucleation_mode_ambient_aerosol_particles_in_air",
        "type": [
          "wcrp:standard-name",
          "cf"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "number-concentration-of-ozone-molecules-in-air",
        "validation-key": "number-concentration-of-ozone-molecules-in-air",
        "ui-label": "Number Concentration Of Ozone Molecules In Air",
        "description": "\"Number concentration\" means the number of particles or other specified objects per unit volume. The chemical formula for ozone is O3. The IUPAC name for ozone is trioxygen.",
        "canonical_units": "m-3",
        "cf-name": "number_concentration_of_ozone_molecules_in_air",
        "type": [
          "wcrp:standard-name",
          "cf"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "number-concentration-of-pm10-aerosol-particles-in-air",
        "validation-key": "number-concentration-of-pm10-aerosol-particles-in-air",
        "ui-label": "Number Concentration Of Pm10 Aerosol Particles In Air",
        "description": "\"Number concentration\" means the number of particles or other specified objects per unit volume. \"Pm10 aerosol\" means atmospheric particulate compounds with an aerodynamic diameter of less than or equal to 10 micrometers. To specify the relative humidity and temperature at which the particle size applies, provide scalar coordinate variables with the standard names of, respectively, \"relative_humidity\" and \"air_temperature\".",
        "canonical_units": "m-3",
        "cf-name": "number_concentration_of_pm10_aerosol_particles_in_air",
        "type": [
          "wcrp:standard-name",
          "cf"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "number-concentration-of-pm2p5-aerosol-particles-in-air",
        "validation-key": "number-concentration-of-pm2p5-aerosol-particles-in-air",
        "ui-label": "Number Concentration Of Pm2p5 Aerosol Particles In Air",
        "description": "\"Number concentration\" means the number of particles or other specified objects per unit volume. \"Pm2p5 aerosol\" means atmospheric particulate compounds with an aerodynamic diameter of less than or equal to 2.5 micrometers. To specify the relative humidity and temperature at which the particle size applies, provide scalar coordinate variables with the standard names of, respectively, \"relative_humidity\" and \"air_temperature\".",
        "canonical_units": "m-3",
        "cf-name": "number_concentration_of_pm2p5_aerosol_particles_in_air",
        "type": [
          "wcrp:standard-name",
          "cf"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "number-concentration-of-stratiform-cloud-liquid-water-particles-at-stratiform-liquid-water-cloud-top",
        "validation-key": "number-concentration-of-stratiform-cloud-liquid-water-particles-at-stratiform-liquid-water-cloud-top",
        "ui-label": "Number Concentration Of Stratiform Cloud Liquid Water Particles At Stratiform Liquid Water Cloud Top",
        "description": "\"Number concentration\" means the number of particles or other specified objects per unit volume. The phrase \"stratiform_liquid_water_cloud_top\" refers to the top of the highest stratiform liquid water cloud. In an atmosphere model, stratiform cloud is that produced by large-scale convergence (not the convection schemes). \"Cloud liquid water\" refers to the liquid phase of cloud water. A diameter of 0.2 mm has been suggested as an upper limit to the size of drops that shall be regarded as cloud drops; larger drops fall rapidly enough so that only very strong updrafts can sustain them. Any such division is somewhat arbitrary, and active cumulus clouds sometimes contain cloud drops much larger than this. Reference: AMS Glossary http://glossary.ametsoc.org/wiki/Cloud_drop.",
        "canonical_units": "m-3",
        "cf-name": "number_concentration_of_stratiform_cloud_liquid_water_particles_at_stratiform_liquid_water_cloud_top",
        "type": [
          "wcrp:standard-name",
          "cf"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "number-of-days-with-air-temperature-above-threshold",
        "validation-key": "number-of-days-with-air-temperature-above-threshold",
        "ui-label": "Number Of Days With Air Temperature Above Threshold",
        "description": "Air temperature is the bulk temperature of the air, not the surface (skin) temperature.  A variable whose standard name has the form number_of_days_with_X_below|above_threshold is a count of the number of days on which the condition X_below|above_threshold is satisfied.  It must have a coordinate variable or scalar coordinate variable with the standard name of X to supply the threshold(s). It must have a climatological time variable, and a cell_methods entry for within days which describes the processing of quantity X before the threshold is applied. A number_of_days is an extensive quantity in time, and the cell_methods entry for over days should be \"sum\".",
        "canonical_units": "1",
        "cf-name": "number_of_days_with_air_temperature_above_threshold",
        "type": [
          "wcrp:standard-name",
          "cf"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "number-of-days-with-air-temperature-below-threshold",
        "validation-key": "number-of-days-with-air-temperature-below-threshold",
        "ui-label": "Number Of Days With Air Temperature Below Threshold",
        "description": "Air temperature is the bulk temperature of the air, not the surface (skin) temperature.  A variable whose standard name has the form number_of_days_with_X_below|above_threshold is a count of the number of days on which the condition X_below|above_threshold is satisfied.  It must have a coordinate variable or scalar coordinate variable with the standard name of X to supply the threshold(s). It must have a climatological time variable, and a cell_methods entry for within days which describes the processing of quantity X before the threshold is applied. A number_of_days is an extensive quantity in time, and the cell_methods entry for over days should be \"sum\".",
        "canonical_units": "1",
        "cf-name": "number_of_days_with_air_temperature_below_threshold",
        "type": [
          "wcrp:standard-name",
          "cf"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "number-of-days-with-lwe-thickness-of-precipitation-amount-above-threshold",
        "validation-key": "number-of-days-with-lwe-thickness-of-precipitation-amount-above-threshold",
        "ui-label": "Number Of Days With Lwe Thickness Of Precipitation Amount Above Threshold",
        "description": "The construction lwe_thickness_of_X_amount or _content means the vertical extent of a layer of liquid water having the same mass per unit area. \"Precipitation\" in the earth's atmosphere means precipitation of water in all phases. The abbreviation \"lwe\" means liquid water equivalent. A variable whose standard name has the form number_of_days_with_X_below|above_threshold is a count of the number of days on which the condition X_below|above_threshold is satisfied. It must have a coordinate variable or scalar coordinate variable with the standard name of X to supply the threshold(s). It must have a climatological time variable, and a cell_methods entry for within days which describes the processing of quantity X before the threshold is applied. A number_of_days is an extensive quantity in time, and the cell_methods entry for over days should be \"sum\".",
        "canonical_units": "1",
        "cf-name": "number_of_days_with_lwe_thickness_of_precipitation_amount_above_threshold",
        "type": [
          "wcrp:standard-name",
          "cf"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "number-of-days-with-surface-temperature-below-threshold",
        "validation-key": "number-of-days-with-surface-temperature-below-threshold",
        "ui-label": "Number Of Days With Surface Temperature Below Threshold",
        "description": "The surface temperature is the temperature at the interface, not the bulk temperature of the medium above or below. The surface called \"surface\" means the lower boundary of the atmosphere. A variable whose standard name has the form number_of_days_with_X_below|above_threshold is a count of the number of days on which the condition X_below|above_threshold is satisfied. It must have a coordinate variable or scalar coordinate variable with the a standard name of X to supply the threshold(s). It must have a climatological time variable, and a cell_methods entry for within days which describes the processing of quantity X before the threshold is applied. A number_of_days is an extensive quantity in time, and the cell_methods entry for over days should be \"sum\".",
        "canonical_units": "1",
        "cf-name": "number_of_days_with_surface_temperature_below_threshold",
        "type": [
          "wcrp:standard-name",
          "cf"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "number-of-days-with-wind-speed-above-threshold",
        "validation-key": "number-of-days-with-wind-speed-above-threshold",
        "ui-label": "Number Of Days With Wind Speed Above Threshold",
        "description": "Wind is defined as a two-dimensional (horizontal) air velocity vector, with no vertical component. (Vertical motion in the atmosphere has the standard name upward_air_velocity.)  The wind speed is the magnitude of the wind velocity.  A variable whose standard name has the form number_of_days_with_X_below|above_threshold is a count of the number of days on which the condition X_below|above_threshold is satisfied.  It must have a coordinate variable or scalar coordinate variable with the standard name of X to supply the threshold(s). It must have a climatological time variable, and a cell_methods entry for within days which describes the processing of quantity X before the threshold is applied. A number_of_days is an extensive quantity in time, and the cell_methods entry for over days should be \"sum\".",
        "canonical_units": "1",
        "cf-name": "number_of_days_with_wind_speed_above_threshold",
        "type": [
          "wcrp:standard-name",
          "cf"
        ]
      },
