      {
        "id": "proportion-of-acceptable-signal-returns-from-acoustic-instrument-in-sea-water",
        "validation-key": "proportion-of-acceptable-signal-returns-from-acoustic-instrument-in-sea-water",
        "ui-label": "Proportion Of Acceptable Signal Returns From Acoustic Instrument In Sea Water",
        "description": "The phrase \"proportion_of_acceptable_signal_returns\" means the fraction of a collection (ensemble) of returned signal transmissions that have passed a set of automatic quality control criteria. For an ADCP (acoustic doppler current profiler) the rejection criteria include low correlation, large error velocity and fish detection. The dimensionless proportion is often but not exclusively expressed as a percentage, when it is referred to as \"percent good\".",
        "canonical_units": "1",
        "cf-name": "proportion_of_acceptable_signal_returns_from_acoustic_instrument_in_sea_water",
        "type": [
          "wcrp:standard-name",
          "cf"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "quality-flag",
        "validation-key": "quality-flag",
        "ui-label": "Quality Flag",
        "description": "A variable with the standard name of quality_flag contains an indication of assessed quality information of another data variable. The linkage between the data variable and the variable or variables with the standard_name of quality_flag is achieved using the ancillary_variables attribute.",
        "canonical_units": "1",
        "cf-name": "quality_flag",
        "type": [
          "wcrp:standard-name",
          "cf"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "radial-sea-water-velocity-away-from-instrument",
        "validation-key": "radial-sea-water-velocity-away-from-instrument",
        "ui-label": "Radial Sea Water Velocity Away From Instrument",
        "description": "A velocity is a vector quantity. \"Radial velocity away from instrument\" means the component of the velocity along the line of sight of the instrument where positive implies movement away from the instrument (i.e. outward). The \"instrument\" (examples are radar and lidar) is the device used to make an observation. A standard name referring to radial velocity \"toward_instrument\" should be used for a data variable having the opposite sign convention.",
        "canonical_units": "m s-1",
        "cf-name": "radial_sea_water_velocity_away_from_instrument",
        "type": [
          "wcrp:standard-name",
          "cf"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "radial-sea-water-velocity-toward-instrument",
        "validation-key": "radial-sea-water-velocity-toward-instrument",
        "ui-label": "Radial Sea Water Velocity Toward Instrument",
        "description": "A velocity is a vector quantity. \"Radial velocity toward instrument\" means the component of the velocity along the line of sight of the instrument where positive implies movement toward the instrument (i.e. inward). The \"instrument\" (examples are radar and lidar) is the device used to make an observation. A standard name referring to radial velocity \"away_from_instrument\" should be used for a data variable having the opposite sign convention.",
        "canonical_units": "m s-1",
        "cf-name": "radial_sea_water_velocity_toward_instrument",
        "type": [
          "wcrp:standard-name",
          "cf"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "radial-velocity-of-scatterers-away-from-instrument",
        "validation-key": "radial-velocity-of-scatterers-away-from-instrument",
        "ui-label": "Radial Velocity Of Scatterers Away From Instrument",
        "description": "A velocity is a vector quantity. \"Radial velocity away from instrument\" means the component of the velocity along the line of sight of the instrument where positive implies movement away from the instrument (i.e. outward). The \"instrument\" (examples are radar and lidar) is the device used to make the observation. The \"scatterers\" are what causes the transmitted signal to be returned to the instrument (examples are aerosols, hydrometeors and refractive index irregularities), of whatever kind the instrument detects. A standard name referring to radial velocity \"toward_instrument\" should be used for a data variable having the opposite sign convention.",
        "canonical_units": "m s-1",
        "cf-name": "radial_velocity_of_scatterers_away_from_instrument",
        "type": [
          "wcrp:standard-name",
          "cf"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "radial-velocity-of-scatterers-toward-instrument",
        "validation-key": "radial-velocity-of-scatterers-toward-instrument",
        "ui-label": "Radial Velocity Of Scatterers Toward Instrument",
        "description": "A velocity is a vector quantity. \"Radial velocity toward instrument\" means the component of the velocity along the line of sight of the instrument where positive implies movement toward the instrument (i.e. inward). The \"instrument\" (examples are radar and lidar) is the device used to make the observation. The \"scatterers\" are what causes the transmitted signal to be returned to the instrument (examples are aerosols, hydrometeors and refractive index irregularities), of whatever kind the instrument detects. A standard name referring to radial velocity \"away_from_instrument\" should be used for a data variable having the opposite sign convention.",
        "canonical_units": "m s-1",
        "cf-name": "radial_velocity_of_scatterers_toward_instrument",
        "type": [
          "wcrp:standard-name",
          "cf"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "radiation-frequency",
